      body: `## Pyrrhus

By the 280s BCE, Rome's power reached the Greek cities of southern Italy. When **Tarentum** (Taranto), the richest of them, came into conflict with Rome, it appealed for help to **Pyrrhus**, king of **Epirus** in northwestern Greece, a relative of Alexander the Great and one of the most brilliant generals of his age. In **280 BCE**, Pyrrhus landed in Italy with about 25,000 men and twenty war **elephants**, the first that the Romans had seen; they called them "Lucanian oxen."

Pyrrhus defeated the Romans at **Heraclea** in 280 and at **Ausculum** in 279. But both victories cost him heavily, and the Romans, unlike the Hellenistic kings he was used to fighting, refused to make peace. When someone congratulated him after Ausculum, he is said to have answered, "**If we win one more battle against the Romans, we shall be utterly ruined**" (Plutarch, *Pyrrhus* 21). This is the origin of the phrase "a **Pyrrhic victory**," a victory won at such cost that it is equivalent to a defeat.

Rome's stubbornness was symbolized by the blind Appius Claudius Caecus, whose speech against peace you read about in lesson 2, and by **Gaius Fabricius Luscinus**, an envoy who, the stories say, was unmoved when Pyrrhus offered him gold and when he tried to frighten him by suddenly revealing an elephant behind a curtain; and who later sent back to Pyrrhus a traitor who offered to poison him (Plutarch, *Pyrrhus* 20–21). After campaigning in Sicily against Carthage, Pyrrhus returned to Italy, was defeated at **Beneventum** in **275 BCE**, and left Italy for good. Tarentum surrendered in 272. Rome was now master of the Italian peninsula south of the Po.

## The Roman alliance

How did Rome control Italy? It did not govern it directly, or tax it. Instead, it bound the Italian communities to itself through a network of **alliances**. Some communities, as we have seen, were incorporated into the Roman state; but most remained nominally independent **allies** (*socii*), each bound to Rome by a separate treaty. The allies governed themselves, kept their own laws, languages, and customs, and paid no tribute. Their one essential obligation was to supply **soldiers** to fight in Rome's wars, at their own expense, under Roman command. They could not make war or alliances with one another, or conduct their own foreign policy.

The system had important advantages for Rome. It gave Rome access to an enormous reserve of manpower. Polybius records a census of the men available for military service in Italy in **225 BCE**, when a Gallic invasion was feared: over **700,000** infantry and **70,000** cavalry, of whom about a third were Romans and the rest allies (2.24). No other Mediterranean state could field such numbers. And the system gave the allies a share in the profits of Rome's wars, in booty and land, while allowing their local elites to keep their power. Because each ally was bound separately to Rome, and many had reasons to fear their neighbors more than Rome, it was difficult for them to unite against Rome.

The system also had a dynamic quality. Every war produced more defeated enemies, who became allies, and so supplied more soldiers for the next war. Some historians, such as **William V. Harris**, in *War and Imperialism in Republican Rome* (1979), have argued that Rome was a deeply militaristic society, in which the nobility's hunger for glory and the people's desire for booty and land drove continual wars of expansion. Others have emphasized Rome's sense of insecurity, or argued that Rome was no more warlike than its neighbors, in a world where war was normal.

## The legion

The instrument of Rome's conquests was the **legion**. In the Republic, the legion was a citizen militia: Roman citizens with enough property to equip themselves were liable for service from seventeen to forty-six years of age, and could be called up for up to sixteen campaigns. Each year, the consuls usually levied four legions, each of about **4,200** infantry and **300** cavalry, together with an equal or larger number of allied troops.

Polybius describes the legion of his day (6.19–26). It was drawn up in **three lines**, according to age and experience:

- the **hastati**, young men, in the front line;
- the **principes**, men in the prime of life, in the second line;
- the **triarii**, veterans, in the third line.

In front of them skirmished the **velites**, the youngest and poorest, with javelins and light shields. Each line was divided into ten **maniples** (*manipuli*, "handfuls") of 120 men (60 for the triarii), arranged in a checkerboard pattern, with gaps between them, so that the lines could advance, retreat, and replace one another. The hastati and principes carried a large oval shield (**scutum**), two heavy javelins (**pila**), which they threw before closing with the enemy, and a short stabbing sword, the **gladius**, adopted, Polybius says, from Spain. The triarii carried thrusting spears. If the first two lines failed, the battle fell back on the triarii; "it has come to the triarii" (*res ad triarios rediit*) was a Roman proverb for a desperate situation (Livy 8.8.11).

The manipular legion was more flexible than the Greek phalanx, which could fight only on level ground and in a single direction. Polybius, a Greek, compared the two at length, and concluded that the legion was superior because the phalanx needed perfect conditions, while the Roman soldier "is fit for every place, every time, and every circumstance" (18.32).

## Discipline

The Roman army was famous for its **discipline**. Every night on campaign, it built a fortified **camp**, always in the same pattern, so that every soldier knew his place. Punishments were severe: a sentry who fell asleep was beaten to death by his comrades, and, as we saw, a unit that fled could be **decimated**. Rewards for courage were public: crowns and decorations, the most prized being the **civic crown**, of oak leaves, for saving the life of a fellow citizen. The army was the school of the Roman citizen, and the source of the Roman nobility's prestige.

> **Pause and consider.** Rome's allies paid no tribute and governed themselves, but had to supply soldiers for Rome's wars. Why might such a system make Rome's expansion almost self-sustaining?`,
