      body: `## Carthage

The conquest of Italy brought Rome face to face with the greatest power of the western Mediterranean: **Carthage**. Founded, according to tradition, by Phoenicians from **Tyre** around **814 BCE**, on the coast of what is now Tunisia, Carthage (Phoenician *Qart-ḥadasht*, "New City") had become a wealthy commercial empire, controlling the coast of North Africa, southern Spain, Sardinia, Corsica, and western Sicily. Its government, which Aristotle admired (*Politics* 2.11), was a republic led by two annual chief magistrates, the **suffetes**, a council of elders, and a popular assembly. Unlike Rome, Carthage relied mainly on **mercenaries** for its armies, but it had the most powerful **navy** in the western Mediterranean. The Romans called the Carthaginians *Poeni*, "Phoenicians," from which comes the adjective **Punic**.

Rome and Carthage had long been on good terms. Polybius records a series of **treaties** between them, the first dated by him to the first year of the Republic, 509 BCE, in which each agreed to respect the other's sphere of influence (3.22–26). But by 264, with Rome in control of southern Italy, only the narrow Strait of Messina separated the two powers.

## The outbreak

The war began over **Messana** (Messina), a Greek city in northeastern Sicily, which had been seized by a band of Italian mercenaries calling themselves the **Mamertines**, "sons of Mars." Under attack by **Hiero**, the king of **Syracuse**, the Mamertines appealed both to Carthage and to Rome. The Roman senate hesitated, but the people, encouraged by the consuls with promises of booty, voted to send help (Polybius 1.10–11). In **264 BCE**, a Roman army crossed into Sicily. It was the first time a Roman army had fought outside Italy. The **First Punic War** (264–241 BCE) had begun.

## Rome takes to the sea

The war soon became a struggle for **Sicily**. Hiero of Syracuse switched sides and became Rome's loyal ally. But the Romans realized that they could not defeat Carthage without a **fleet**. They had little experience of naval warfare. According to Polybius, they built a fleet of a hundred **quinqueremes** (warships with five files of oarsmen) and twenty triremes, using a stranded Carthaginian warship as a model, and trained the rowers on benches set up on land (1.20–21); Pliny the Elder says the fleet was afloat sixty days after the timber was cut (*Natural History* 16.192). (In 1969 and 1971, the remains of what are thought to be two Carthaginian warships of this period were found off Marsala, in western Sicily, built with letters painted on the timbers to guide assembly, which suggests a form of mass production.)

To make up for their inexperience, the Romans invented a device called the **corvus**, the "raven": a boarding bridge, about eleven meters long, with a heavy spike at its end, which was dropped onto the deck of an enemy ship, pinning it, so that Roman soldiers could cross and fight as if on land (Polybius 1.22). With it, the Romans won their first great naval victory at **Mylae** in **260 BCE**. The consul **Gaius Duilius**, who commanded, was honored with a column in the Forum decorated with the bronze beaks (*rostra*) of captured ships; a later copy of its inscription survives.

## Disaster and endurance

In **256 BCE**, the Romans won a great naval battle at **Ecnomus**, off southern Sicily, perhaps the largest naval battle in ancient history, with hundreds of ships on each side, and invaded **Africa**. The consul **Marcus Atilius Regulus** won several victories, but offered harsh terms of peace, which the Carthaginians rejected. They hired a Spartan mercenary general, **Xanthippus**, who reorganized their army and defeated and captured Regulus in 255. Later Roman legend told that Regulus was sent to Rome on parole to negotiate an exchange of prisoners, advised the senate to refuse, and then, keeping his oath, returned to Carthage, knowing that he would be tortured to death. The story, celebrated by Horace (*Odes* 3.5), may be invented.

The Romans also suffered terrible losses at sea, from storms more than from the enemy. In 255, a fleet returning from Africa was caught in a storm off southern Sicily, and, according to Polybius, of 364 ships, only 80 survived (1.37). Further fleets were lost in 253 and 249. Some historians think the weight of the corvus made the ships unstable. In **249 BCE**, at **Drepana**, the consul **Publius Claudius Pulcher** was defeated after, the story goes, he ignored an unfavorable omen: when the sacred chickens refused to eat, he had them thrown overboard, saying, "Let them drink, since they will not eat" (Cicero, *On the Nature of the Gods* 2.7; Suetonius, *Tiberius* 2).

The Carthaginian general **Hamilcar Barca** fought a long guerrilla war in western Sicily. Rome was exhausted, and its treasury empty. But in 242, a new fleet of two hundred ships was built with money loaned by private citizens, and in **241 BCE**, it defeated the Carthaginian fleet at the **Aegates Islands**, off western Sicily. In recent years, underwater archaeologists have recovered from the site many bronze rams from warships, some bearing Latin inscriptions naming Roman magistrates, others with Punic inscriptions.

## The peace

Carthage made peace. It agreed to evacuate Sicily and to pay an indemnity of 3,200 talents over ten years (Polybius 1.62–63). Sicily became Rome's first **province** (*provincia*), governed by a Roman magistrate and paying tribute, a new form of rule outside Italy. A few years later, in **238 BCE**, while Carthage was fighting a desperate war against its own unpaid mercenaries, Rome seized **Sardinia** and **Corsica** as well, and demanded an additional indemnity. Polybius, usually sympathetic to Rome, condemned this as unjust, and believed that it was one of the causes of the next war (3.10, 3.28).

Polybius summed up the First Punic War as the longest, most continuous, and greatest war he knew of, and judged that the Romans had won through their **determination**: "the Romans, in general, rely on force in all their undertakings, and think it incumbent on them to carry out their purposes in spite of everything" (1.37.7).

> **Pause and consider.** Rome lost hundreds of ships and tens of thousands of men in storms, yet kept building fleets until it won. What does this suggest about the sources of Roman success? Is such determination always a virtue?`,
